Lun\">Liu Lun</a></div><div class=\"wst-header-forward-arrow\">\u2192</div></div></div><div class=\"wst-header-notes header&#95;notes searchaux wst-unknown-notes\"><style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14791120\">.mw-parser-output .plainSister{display:block;font-size:93%;line-height:normal;list-style-type:none;list-style-image:none;list-style-position:outside;border:1px solid var(--border-color-interactive--hover,#AAA);float:right;clear:right;margin:0.5ex 0 0.5ex 0.5ex;padding:0;background:var(--background-color-base,#FFFFFF);color:var(--color-base,#202122)}.mw-parser-output .sisicon,.mw-parser-output .dabicon{padding-right:1ex}</style><ul class=\"plainSister\"><li class=\"sisitem\"><span class=\"sisicon\"><span typeof=\"mw:File/Frameless\"><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\"><img alt=\"Sister Projects\" src=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/20px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"18\" height=\"18\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Wikimedia-logo.svg/40px-Wikimedia-logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikisource.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"1024\" data-file-height=\"1024\" /></a></span></span><a href=\"/wiki/Special:SiteMatrix\" title=\"Special:SiteMatrix\">sister projects</a>: <a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q107367447\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q107367447\">Wikidata item</a></li></ul></div><div class=\"ws-noexport\" id=\"ws-data\" style=\"speak:none\"><span id=\"ws-article-id\">3645516</span><span id=\"ws-title\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period\">Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period</a>, Volume 1 \u2014 Liu Liang-tso</span><span id=\"ws-editor\">Arthur W. Hummel</span><span id=\"ws-author\">Earl Swisher</span></div></div>\n<p><span style=\"display:none\" id=\"dynamic&#95;layout&#95;overrider\">Layout 2</span>\n</p>\n<div class=\"prp-pages-output\" lang=\"en\">\n<p><span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"524\" data-page-number=\"524\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/536\" data-page-index=\"536\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/536\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;536\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span><b>LIU</b> Liang-tso \u5289\u826f\u4f50 (<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r14767791\">.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip{cursor:help;border-bottom:thin dotted cornflowerblue}.mw-parser-output .wst-tooltip-nodash{border-bottom:none}</style><span class=\"wst-tooltip wst-tooltip-dash\" title=\"Courtesy name (Tz\u00fb/Zi, \u5b57)\">T.</span>&#160;\u660e\u8f14), d. 1667, renegade Ming general, was a native of Tatung, Shansi. He was associated first with <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Kao_Chieh\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Kao Chieh\">Kao Chieh</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] under the rebel, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Li_Tz%C5%AD-ch%27%C3%AAng\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch&#39;\u00eang\">Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], and later with the Ming brigade general, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Huang_T%C3%AA-kung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Huang T\u00ea-kung\">Huang T\u00ea-kung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], against <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chang_Hsien-chung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chang Hsien-chung\">Chang Hsien-chung</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>]. After the dynasty fell and the Prince of Fu (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Chu_Yu-sung\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Chu Yu-sung\">Chu Yu-sung</a></span>) had set up his court in&#32;<span><span class=\"pagenum ws-pagenum\" id=\"525\" data-page-number=\"525\" data-page-name=\"Page:Eminent Chinese Of The Ch\u2019ing Period - Hummel - 1943 - Vol. 1.pdf/537\" data-page-index=\"537\" data-page-quality=\"3\" title=\"Page:Eminent&#95;Chinese&#95;Of&#95;The&#95;Ch%E2%80%99ing&#95;Period&#95;-&#95;Hummel&#95;-&#95;1943&#95;-&#95;Vol.&#95;1.pdf/537\"><span id=\"pageindex&#95;537\" class=\"pagenum-inner ws-noexport\">&#8203;</span></span></span>Nanking, Liu Liang-tso with his two former associates and <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Liu_Ts%C3%AA-ch%27ing\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Liu Ts\u00ea-ch&#39;ing\">Liu Ts\u00ea-ch'ing</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>] were called the Four Guardian Generals (\u56db\u93ad). He was made earl of Kuang-ch'ang (\u5ee3\u660c\u4f2f) and assigned to the Anhwei-Honan sector with residence at Lin-huai. Later he was transferred to Hs\u00fcchou in northern Kiangsu. In 1645, when the Manchu prince, <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Dodo\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Dodo\">Dodo</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], came south, Liu Liang-tso surrendered with 100,000 men. He retained office under the Ch'ing and led an expedition to subdue Kiangsu. This province pacified, he went to Peking, was assigned to the Chinese Bordered Yellow Banner, and in 1648 was given the rank of viscount of the second class. For his services with Tantai (see under <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Yangg%C3%BBri_efu\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Yangg\u00fbri efu\">Yangg\u00fbri</a></span>) in a southern expedition he was made Junior Assistant Chamberlain of the Imperial Bodyguard. In 1661 he was appointed general-in-chief of Kiangnan and Kiangan. Later in the same year he was transferred to Chihli. In 1666 he resigned on account of old age and died the following year. His son, Liu Ts\u00ea-han \u5289\u6fa4\u6db5, inherited his rank of viscount, a rank that was abolished in 1734.\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431518\">.mw-parser-output .wst-rule{background-color:currentcolor;color:inherit;width:auto;margin:2px auto 2px auto;height:1px;overflow:hidden}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-left{margin-left:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.wst-rule-right{margin-right:0}.mw-parser-output .wst-rule.__uirule{background-color:#a2a9b1;color:inherit;border:0;margin-top:0.2em;margin-bottom:0.2em}</style><hr class=\"wst-rule wst-rule-left\" style=\"color:inherit;width:40%;\" />\n<p>[1/254/1b; 2/79/16a; \u570b\u53f2\u8cb3\u81e3\u50b3 <i>Kuo-shih \u00ear-ch'\u00ean chuan</i> 7/8b; <span class=\"ws-authlkpl\"><a href=\"/wiki/Eminent_Chinese_of_the_Ch%27ing_Period/Hs%C3%BC_Tz%C5%AD\" title=\"Eminent Chinese of the Ch&#39;ing Period/Hs\u00fc Tz\u016d\">Hs\u00fc Tz\u016d</a></span>&#32;[<i>q.&#8239;v.</i>], <i>Hsiao-t'ien chi-chuan</i> 64/12a.]\n</p>\n<style data-mw-deduplicate=\"TemplateStyles:r15431519\">.mw-parser-output .wst-right{text-align:right}</style><div class=\"wst-right\" style=\"margin-right:2em;\">\n<p><span class=\"smallcaps\" style=\"font-variant:small-caps;\">Earl Swisher</span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<p><br />\n&#32;\n</p>\n</div>\n</div>"},"links":[{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chang Hsien-chung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Chu Yu-sung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Dodo"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Hs\u00fc Tz\u016d"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Huang T\u00ea-kung"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Kao Chieh"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Li Tz\u016d-ch'\u00eang"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liu K'un-i"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liu Lun"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Liu Ts\u00ea-ch'ing"},{"ns":0,"exists":"","*":"Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period/Yangg\u00fbri efu"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Arthur W. Hummel"},{"ns":102,"exists":"","*":"Author:Earl Swisher"}],"sections":[],"wikitext":{"*":"{{Eminent Chinese of the Ch'ing Period\n | volume   = 1\n | onlysection = liu liang-tso\n | previous = Liu K'un-i\n | next     = Liu Lun\n | from     = 536\n | to       = 537\n | notes    =\n | section_author = Earl Swisher\n}}{{DEFAULTSORT:Liu Liang-tso}}"}}}